 this as a land  of opportunity  uh as millions of people have uh  throughout the 200 years  and um but there's a but there's a  process right  and uh and and and it is like the other  speaker  uh was saying it for those of us that  have done it legally  it's kind of a slap slap in the face and  for those that are going through the  process  i'm sure they're very very frustrated  how uh you know they've  done everything right only to have uh  an administration you know open the  border and just allow folks to  to come in and um but really at the end  of the day  uh it's it's it's the problem is a lot  bigger than just  is it a fairness issue and what what i  think about it or what other folks that  are  that are doing it correctly it also  affects many  americans that that may be american-born  right uh  the labor pool um uh for example  gets uh uh you know uh is  is affected by the surge of immigrants  human trafficking where i live in in san  diego county  uh it is a hub for human trafficking and  and so you gotta also look at the at the  human suffrage that's going on  and that's created by some of these  policies uh that's what should be  keeping people up at life at night not  whether  it's fair for me as a as an immigrant  came in  legally but what's happening to those  those those kids  and adults as they are captured in these  nets of human trafficking  as they make their way across  well yeah i think that there is  bipartisan support for  dreamers but you know congressman steve  scalise says that right now as we're  facing such a border crisis  this would send the wrong signal because  then more you know more people will  want to come over and and these two  bills um although they just passed in in  the house will have a more difficult  time in the senate  senator dick durbin did talk about these  bills he said a legislative solution  is long overdue for dreamers who have  devoted their lives to our country  undocumented migrant farm workers doing  back breaking agriculture work  during this pandemic to keep food on our  table and tps recipients  who have built their lives here and are  making essential contributions  to our national recovery emma pola can  you respond to that  yes i'd like to say that it nevertheless  it's uh  insulting to the people that have waited  years to be able to come  and to be um that have gone through the  process of  studying all of the the constitution  the bill of rights the declaration of  independence etc to go through the test  so they can become citizens and be able  to vote  it is a privilege and it is not treated  like a privilege  with this bill they will be able to vote  almost right away  or they can vote illegally and not be  persecuted  i think prosecuted that is and and it's  also  a tremendous um slap in the face for  uh angel moms that have lost children  too  illegal and i know that speaks that that  you know agnes unfortunately is one of  them um  unfortunately we ran out of time and  your perspective  all of your opinions are so important  because i do feel like  the folks who migrated here legally came  here legally are a forgotten part of  this conversation  so thank you so much for sharing your  perspective with us this morning  we appreciate it thank you okay we'll be  right back

